Stage 1 – Master oscillator (burst core)
Parts:

1× 2N3904 (Q1)

1× 3.579545 MHz NTSC crystal

1× 1 MΩ (base–collector)

1× 100 kΩ (base–ground)

2× 22–33 pF (crystal load caps)

Build:

Wire Q1 as a Pierce oscillator:

Crystal between collector and base node.

1 MΩ from collector to base.

100 kΩ from base to ground.

Two caps from crystal ends to ground.

Power Q1 from +5 V via a 10 kΩ collector resistor.

Goal: Stable ~3.58 MHz at CRYSTAL_OSC_OUT.

Stage 2 – Divider chain (frame/field/phase taps)
Parts (per stage):

3–4× 2N3904 (Q2–Q5)

Per stage: 10 kΩ collector resistor, 47 kΩ base resistor, 1 nF to ground (optional shaping).

Build:

Cascade transistor flip‑flops or simple multivibrators from CRYSTAL_OSC_OUT.

Aim for rough ladder:

Stage 1: ~358 kHz

Stage 2: ~35.8 kHz

Stage 3: ~3.58 kHz

Stage 4: ~358 Hz

Derive:

FR_30 ≈ 30 Hz from 358 Hz via extra divide.

FR_100 ≈ 100 Hz tap.

FR_1000 ≈ 1 kHz tap.

FIELD_SYNC ≈ 60 Hz.

PHASE_CLK ≈ 15 kHz.

Goal: Clean logic‑level pulses at those nodes.

Stage 3 – Mode selector (frame‑rate)
Parts:

3× 2N3904 (QM1–QM3)

3× 10 kΩ collector resistors

3× 4.7–10 kΩ base resistors (from MCU pins)

Build:

Connect:

QM1 collector to FR_30

QM2 collector to FR_100

QM3 collector to FR_1000

Tie all emitters to ground.

OR their collectors together to FRAME_CLK_OUT via small resistor if needed.

Drive bases from MODE_SEL[1:0] decode so only one QM is ON at a time.

Goal: FRAME_CLK_OUT switches between 30/100/1000 Hz under MCU control.

Stage 4 – Illusion class selector (pulse‑width/phase networks)
Parts:

7× 2N3904 (QC0–QC6)

7× RC networks (one per class L0–L6)

7× 4.7–10 kΩ base resistors

Build:

For each class:

Make an RC network from FRAME_CLK_OUT to a node PW_Lx_NODE with:

L0: 100 kΩ + 10 µF

L1: 100 kΩ + 8.2 µF

L2: 100 kΩ + 6.8 µF

L3: 100 kΩ + 4.7 µF

L4: 47 kΩ + 3.3 µF

L5: 47 kΩ + 2.2 µF

L6: 47 kΩ + 1 µF

Each QCx:

Collector to its PW_Lx_NODE.

Emitters tied together to PULSE_CTRL_OUT.

Bases driven from CLASS_SEL[2:0] decode (one‑hot).

Goal: PULSE_CTRL_OUT gets a class‑dependent pulse width/phase.

Stage 5 – Profile selector + envelopes
Parts:

4× 2N3904 (QP0–QP3)

RC envelopes:

Breathing: 100 kΩ + 10 µF

Drift: 100 kΩ + 4.7–10 µF

Ghostwave: 47 kΩ + 4.7 µF

Whiteout: 10 kΩ + 1 µF

4× 4.7–10 kΩ base resistors

Build:

Drive each envelope from FRAME_CLK_OUT or FIELD_SYNC into its RC node.

QP0–QP3:

Collectors to each envelope node.

Emitters tied to LUMA_ENV_IN.

Bases driven from PROFILE_SEL[1:0] decode.

Goal: LUMA_ENV_IN carries the chosen profile’s luma envelope.

Stage 6 – Dot driver + CRT/LED interface
Parts:

1× 2N2222 or 2N3904 (QD1)

1× 4.7 kΩ base resistor

1× 1–2.2 kΩ output resistor

CRT video input or LED + series resistor

Build:

Combine PULSE_CTRL_OUT and LUMA_ENV_IN (simple summing node or gating) into QD1 base via 4.7 kΩ.

QD1 emitter to ground.

QD1 collector to CRT video input through 1–2.2 kΩ, or to LED + resistor.

Goal: Single dot whose brightness and timing follow the illusion engine.

Stage 7 – MCU hook
Parts:

Small MCU (e.g. ATtiny/Arduino Nano)

7× 4.7–10 kΩ resistors (to selector bases)

Build:

Input:

PIN_FIELD_SYNC from FIELD_SYNC node (via 10 kΩ, optional buffer).

Outputs:

MODE_0/1 → mode selector base network.

CLASS_0/1/2 → class selector decode.

PROFILE_0/1 → profile selector decode.

Use the code we wrote to:

Attach interrupt on FIELD_SYNC.

Flip pins only when safeToSwitch is true.

Goal: Clean, field‑aligned switching of mode/class/profile without disturbing analog timing.
